    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 160505\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1005 PM PDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated thunderstorms will be possible across Mohave County\n  Wednesday afternoon and evening, with gusty winds and localized heavy\n  rainfall possible. \n\n* Elsewhere, breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts will continue \n  through the week with dry conditions and near-normal \n  temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through early next week.\n\nDespite respectable northward moisture advection this afternoon,\nwarm mid-level temperatures are keeping the region capped and\nstable with some just some scattered cumulus fields across our\neastern zones. Surface dewpoints have climbed into the low to mid\n50s across southern Nevada and northwest Arizona, and even into\nthe mid-60s along the Colorado River Valley. As a closed low drops\ninto Northern California and the Great Basin on Wednesday,\nnorthward moisture transport will continue and a few storms may \nfire up across Mohave County during the afternoon and evening \nhours as favorable jet dynamics help overcome the persistent \ncapping. Storms should tend to move swiftly thanks to elevated \nwind shear in place, but training storms will have the potential \nfor localized flooding, and the enhanced shear could result in a \nfew stronger storms capable of small hail and strong wind gusts. \n\nElsewhere, dry conditions are expected to continue as dry\nsouthwesterly flow keeps monsoonal moisture focused further east\ninto Arizona. Daily gusty winds can be expected with afternoon\ngusts of 25 to 35 mph expected through Wednesday evening, trending\nslightly weaker Thursday. The flow pattern will flatten out over\nthe weekend with wind speeds easing area wide and dry and\nseasonable weather continuing. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nsoutherly winds tonight give way to another round of gusty south-\nsoutheast breezes Wednesday morning, gradually turning to a true \nsouth direction in the afternoon. On average, wind direction during \nfrom 16z-22z will be ~160, but brief periods as far east as 120 are \npossible. Gusts of 20-30 knots are likely (70%) from 17z - 04z. \nWinds weaken after sunset. Few to scattered clouds around 10kft with \ntemperatures remaining below 100F.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds tonight with \nanother round of gusty south-southeast breezes on Wednesday in most \nlocations. Gusts generally 20-30 knots, but would not be surprised \nto see a few ~35 knot readings in the Owens Valley and lower \nColorado River Valley. The latter will likely be induced by outflow \nfrom thunderstorms expected in western AZ. Best storm chances (40-\n60%) are in far southern and northern Mohave County Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. The more robust convection will be capable of \nproducing strong outflow winds, hail, and heavy rain. Few to \nscattered clouds around 10kft, with greater coverage in locations \nwith the best storm chances.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
